\title{Injectivity Is All You Need:\\
A Unified Single-Feature Universal Approximation Theorem\\
for Standard Machine-Learning Activations}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-27}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We give a unified, minimal account of universal approximation for the standard activation
functions of machine learning. The classical folklore states that single-hidden-layer
networks with sigmoidal activations are universal approximators; numerous variants
re-prove this separately for the logistic sigmoid, hyperbolic tangent, softplus, and
arctangent, each invoking properties peculiar to the chosen activation. We show that all of
these are instances of a single structural fact: a strictly monotone (hence injective)
continuous activation, composed with any injective continuous input feature, generates a
uniformly dense subalgebra of the continuous functions on every compact domain. The proof
reduces, via the Stone--Weierstrass theorem, to the elementary observation that one
injective function already separates points. We isolate the lemma that composition
preserves injectivity, derive strict monotonicity for each of the four canonical
activations, and obtain---as corollaries of one general theorem---the density of
single-activation polynomial read-out networks on any compact interval. We also identify the
sharp boundary of the phenomenon: non-injective activations (e.g.\ Gaussian/radial ones) can
fail to be universal as a single feature, so a \emph{family} of units is genuinely required.
The development is fully formalized and machine-checked. We discuss the separation between the
qualitative density question (settled here, governed entirely by injectivity) and the
quantitative approximation-rate question (activation-dependent, future work), and sketch
explicit shallow-network rate bounds.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} universal approximation, Stone--Weierstrass theorem, sigmoid,
tanh, softplus, arctan, strict monotonicity, injectivity, point separation, exponential
polynomials, EML class, dense subalgebra.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Background and motivation}
The universal approximation theorem is the theoretical bedrock of neural network modelling.
In its classical forms (Cybenko, Hornik, Funahashi, and others) it asserts that finite
linear combinations of activated affine functions are dense in the space of continuous
functions on a compact set, for suitable choices of activation. The result licenses the
practitioner's belief that a sufficiently large network can represent any reasonable target.

The literature presents universality as a property tied to specific activation functions and
proven by activation-specific techniques: the logistic sigmoid via its sigmoidal limits; the
hyperbolic tangent via its odd symmetry and saturation; softplus via smoothing arguments.
This proliferation obscures a simpler organizing principle.

This paper makes that principle explicit, working in the
\emph{EML (Exponential--Multiplicative--Logarithmic)} approximation programme, whose abstract
Stone--Weierstrass core reduces uniform density of a generated subalgebra to point separation.
Within this framework, the entire qualitative content of single-activation universality is the
\emph{injectivity} of the activation, which for the standard zoo follows from \emph{strict
monotonicity}.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item A composition lemma (\code{injective\_comp}): the composition of injective continuous
maps is injective.
\item A general single-activation density theorem (\code{activation\_feature\_dense},
\code{activation\_feature\_approx}).
\item A strict-monotonicity interface (\code{strictMono\_feature\_dense}) turning each
activation into a one-line check.
\item Concrete instances for the logistic sigmoid, softplus, tanh, and arctan
(\code{strictMono\_sigmoid}, \code{strictMono\_softplus}, \code{strictMono\_tanh},
\code{Real.arctan\_strictMono}), and the resulting interval density theorems
(\code{sigmoid\_dense\_Icc}, \code{softplus\_dense\_Icc}, \code{tanh\_dense\_Icc},
\code{arctan\_dense\_Icc}), unified by \code{activation\_dense\_Icc}.
\item A foundational corollary: density of exponential polynomials
(\code{exponentialPolynomials\_dense\_Icc}, \code{exp\_monomials\_span\_dense}).
\item A sharp boundary: non-injective activations can fail single-feature universality, so
injectivity is essentially necessary.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{What is and is not claimed}
We settle the \emph{qualitative} question---can a single-activation polynomial read-out hit
any continuous target to arbitrary accuracy on a compact domain? Yes, and the reason is
injectivity, uniformly across activations. We do not here resolve the \emph{quantitative}
question of approximation rates (degree/width as a function of accuracy and smoothness); that
is genuinely activation-dependent and is the subject of Section~\ref{sec:rates}.

\section{Preliminaries and notation}
Throughout, $X$ is a topological space; for the density results $X$ is additionally compact.
We write $C(X,\R)$ for the continuous real-valued functions on $X$ with the supremum norm
$\norm{f}_\infty = \sup_{x\in X}\lvert f(x)\rvert$, finite when $X$ is compact. The space
$C(X,\R)$ is a commutative $\R$-algebra under pointwise operations.

For $S \subseteq C(X,\R)$, the generated subalgebra $\adjoin_{\R} S$ is the smallest
$\R$-subalgebra containing $S$. For a single generator $g$,
\[
  \adjoin_{\R}\{g\} = \Big\{ \textstyle\sum_{k=0}^{d} c_k\, g^{k} : d\in\N,\ c_k\in\R \Big\}.
\]
A family $A \subseteq C(X,\R)$ \emph{separates points} if for all $x \ne y$ there exists
$h \in A$ with $h(x) \ne h(y)$. A map $h$ is \emph{injective} if $h(x)=h(y)\Rightarrow x=y$;
$\sigma:\R\to\R$ is \emph{strictly monotone (increasing)} if $x<y$ implies
$\sigma(x)<\sigma(y)$, and every strictly increasing function is injective.

\section{The analytic engine: Stone--Weierstrass and point separation}

\begin{theorem}[Stone--Weierstrass, density form]\label{thm:sw}
Let $X$ be compact and $A \le C(X,\R)$ a subalgebra that separates points. Then $A$ is
uniformly dense in $C(X,\R)$; equivalently $\overline{A} = C(X,\R)$.
\end{theorem}

\noindent In the formalization this is \code{eml\_topologicalClosure\_eq\_top\_of\_separatesPoints},
with $\varepsilon$-form \code{eml\_exists\_uniform\_approx}: for every $f$ and
$\varepsilon>0$ there is $p \in A$ with $\norm{p-f}_\infty < \varepsilon$.

\begin{lemma}[single injective member separates;
\code{separatesPoints\_of\_injective\_mem}]\label{lem:sep}
Let $A \le C(X,\R)$ be a subalgebra and $g \in A$ injective. Then $A$ separates points.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $x \ne y$. Injectivity gives $g(x)\ne g(y)$, and $g\in A$ is the witness.
\end{proof}

\section{Single-generator universality}

\begin{theorem}[single-generator density; \code{adjoin\_singleton\_dense}]\label{thm:single}
Let $X$ be compact and $g\in C(X,\R)$ injective. Then
$\overline{\adjoin_{\R}\{g\}} = C(X,\R)$; the polynomials in $g$ are uniformly dense.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The generator $g$ lies in $\adjoin_\R\{g\}$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:sep} the subalgebra separates
points; by Theorem~\ref{thm:sw} its closure is everything.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$\varepsilon$-form; \code{adjoin\_singleton\_approx}]
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:single}, for every $f\in C(X,\R)$ and
$\varepsilon>0$ there is a polynomial $p$ in $g$ with $\norm{p-f}_\infty < \varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Composition preserves injectivity}

\begin{lemma}[\code{injective\_comp}]\label{lem:comp}
Let $\sigma:\R\to\R$ be continuous and injective and $g:X\to\R$ continuous and injective.
Then $\sigma\circ g \in C(X,\R)$ is injective.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $\sigma(g(x))=\sigma(g(y))$ then injectivity of $\sigma$ gives $g(x)=g(y)$, and
injectivity of $g$ gives $x=y$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[single-activation universality;
\code{activation\_feature\_dense}]\label{thm:act}
Let $X$ be compact, $\sigma\in C(\R,\R)$ injective, and $g\in C(X,\R)$ injective. Then
$\overline{\adjoin_{\R}\{\sigma\circ g\}} = C(X,\R)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:comp}, $\sigma\circ g$ is injective; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:single}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$\varepsilon$-form; \code{activation\_feature\_approx}]
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:act}, for every $f\in C(X,\R)$ and $\varepsilon>0$
there is a polynomial $p$ in $\sigma\circ g$ with $\norm{p-f}_\infty < \varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[strict-monotone interface; \code{strictMono\_feature\_dense}]
Let $\sigma:\R\to\R$ be strictly monotone and continuous, and $g\in C(X,\R)$ injective on
compact $X$. Then $\sigma\circ g$ generates a dense subalgebra of $C(X,\R)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Strict monotonicity implies injectivity (\code{StrictMono.injective}); apply
Theorem~\ref{thm:act}.
\end{proof}

\section{The standard activation zoo}

\subsection{Logistic sigmoid}
$\sigma(x) = \dfrac{1}{1+e^{-x}}$. Continuous (\code{continuous\_sigmoid}: positive
denominator). Strictly increasing (\code{strictMono\_sigmoid}: $x<y \Rightarrow e^{-y}<e^{-x}
\Rightarrow 1+e^{-y}<1+e^{-x} \Rightarrow \sigma(x)<\sigma(y)$). Injective
(\code{injective\_sigmoid}). Continuous-map form \code{sigmoidCM}.

\subsection{Softplus}
$s(x) = \log(1+e^{x})$. Continuous (\code{continuous\_softplus}). Strictly increasing
(\code{strictMono\_softplus}: $e^x<e^y\Rightarrow 1+e^x<1+e^y$, and $\log$ is strictly
increasing on positives). Injective (\code{injective\_softplus}). Form \code{softplusCM}.

\subsection{Hyperbolic tangent}
$\tanh(x) = \dfrac{\sinh x}{\cosh x}$. Continuous (\code{continuous\_tanh}; $\cosh>0$).
Strictly increasing (\code{strictMono\_tanh}: $\frac{d}{dx}\tanh x = 1/\cosh^2 x > 0$, and
\code{strictMono\_of\_deriv\_pos}). Injective (\code{injective\_tanh}). Form \code{tanhCM}.

\subsection{Arctangent}
$\arctan(x):\R\to(-\tfrac\pi2,\tfrac\pi2)$. Continuous (\code{Real.continuous\_arctan}).
Strictly increasing (\code{Real.arctan\_strictMono}). Injective (\code{injective\_arctan}).
Form \code{arctanCM}.

\section{Density on a compact interval}
Specializing to $X=[a,b]$ with the coordinate feature $g=\mathrm{id}$ (\code{iccCoord},
injective by \code{injective\_iccCoord}):

\begin{corollary}[\code{sigmoid\_dense\_Icc}]
On any compact interval $[a,b]$, the polynomials in $\sigma(x)=1/(1+e^{-x})$ are uniformly
dense in $C([a,b],\R)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{corollary}[\code{softplus\_dense\_Icc}]
On any compact interval $[a,b]$, the polynomials in $s(x)=\log(1+e^{x})$ are uniformly dense
in $C([a,b],\R)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{corollary}[\code{tanh\_dense\_Icc}]
On any compact interval $[a,b]$, the polynomials in $\tanh(x)$ are uniformly dense in
$C([a,b],\R)$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{corollary}[\code{arctan\_dense\_Icc}]
On any compact interval $[a,b]$, the polynomials in $\arctan(x)$ are uniformly dense in
$C([a,b],\R)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[uniform statement; \code{activation\_dense\_Icc}]
Let $\sigma:\R\to\R$ be strictly monotone and continuous, $[a,b]$ a compact interval. Then
the polynomials in $\sigma$ are uniformly dense in $C([a,b],\R)$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{The exponential ancestor}
\begin{theorem}[exponential polynomials; \code{exponentialPolynomials\_dense\_Icc}]
On any compact interval $[a,b]$, the subalgebra generated by $x\mapsto e^{x}$---finite real
combinations $\sum_k c_k e^{k x}$---is uniformly dense in $C([a,b],\R)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{theorem}[span form; \code{exp\_monomials\_span\_dense}]
For compact $X$ and injective $g$, the linear span of $\{(\exp\circ g)^k : k\in\N\}$ is dense
in $C(X,\R)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof idea]
The carrier of $\adjoin_\R\{h\}$ equals the linear span of the powers $\{h^k\}$
(\code{adjoin\_singleton\_coe\_eq\_span\_pow}); transport Theorem~\ref{thm:single}.
\end{proof}

\section{Approximation rates for shallow networks}\label{sec:rates}
Density is qualitative. The quantitative refinement: to achieve uniform error $\varepsilon$
for an $L$-Lipschitz target $f$ on $[a,b]$, what polynomial degree $d$ suffices? A clean
route:

\textbf{Step 1 (change of variable).} With $u=\sigma(x)$, $\sigma$ a strictly monotone
continuous bijection from $[a,b]$ onto $[\sigma(a),\sigma(b)]$, approximating $f(x)$ by a
degree-$d$ polynomial in $\sigma(x)$ equals approximating $F(u):=f(\sigma^{-1}(u))$ by an
ordinary degree-$d$ polynomial in $u$.

\textbf{Step 2 (classical rates).} Jackson's theorem: if $F$ is $L_F$-Lipschitz on an
interval of length $M=\sigma(b)-\sigma(a)$,
\[
  \inf_{\deg p \le d}\ \norm{F-p}_\infty \ \le\ \frac{C\,L_F\,M}{d},
\]
improving to $O(d^{-r})$ for $C^r$ targets and geometric $O(\rho^{-d})$ for analytic ones.
The activation enters through the modulus distortion: $L_F$ depends on $1/\sigma'$ and $M$ on
the range of $\sigma$. Saturating activations (sigmoid, tanh, arctan) have bounded range but
steep inverse near the endpoints; softplus is non-saturating on the right with milder inverse
slope but unbounded range. Formalizing these rate theorems is concrete future work.

\section{The sharp boundary: when one neuron is not enough}

\begin{proposition}[non-injective collapse, informal]
Let $\sigma:\R\to\R$ be continuous with $\sigma(a)=\sigma(b)$ for some $a\ne b$. Take
$X=\{a,b\}$ and $g=\mathrm{id}$. Then $\sigma\circ g$ is constant on $X$, so
$\adjoin_\R\{\sigma\circ g\}$ consists of constants and fails to separate $a,b$; its closure
is not all of $C(X,\R)$.
\end{proposition}

This rules out radial/Gaussian activations $\rho(x)=e^{-x^2}$ (even) as single-feature
universal approximators; one then needs a \emph{family} of units. Combined with
Theorem~\ref{thm:act}, this suggests the sharp characterization: a continuous activation is
single-feature universal for every compact $X$ and injective $g$ iff it is injective.

\section{Related work}
Classical universal approximation theorems (Cybenko 1989; Hornik--Stinchcombe--White 1989;
Funahashi 1989; Leshno--Lin--Pinkus--Schocken 1993) establish density for broad activation
classes. The Leshno et al.\ characterization---a continuous activation yields universal
shallow networks iff it is non-polynomial---concerns the multi-unit ridge setting. Our
statement is orthogonal: we fix a single feature and obtain universality through polynomial
read-out; the governing property is then injectivity, not non-polynomiality. The two
viewpoints meet in the EML programme's abstract Stone--Weierstrass core.

\section{Conclusion}
Universal approximation for the standard activation zoo is not four theorems but one. The
load-bearing property is injectivity, supplied by strict monotonicity; the analytic content
is the Stone--Weierstrass reduction of density to point separation; the glue is that injective
maps compose. We recover, as one-line corollaries, density of single-neuron polynomial
read-out networks built on the logistic sigmoid, softplus, tanh, and arctan, with the
exponential as the first instance. Dropping injectivity, a single neuron can fail to be
universal. What remains activation-dependent is the rate of approximation, the natural subject
of future quantitative work.

\section*{Future directions}
\begin{description}[leftmargin=*]
\item[C1.] Non-injective bounded activations require a family: a Gaussian $\rho(x)=e^{-x^2}$
is even, so a single RBF feature cannot separate a value from its negation; exhibit a compact
$X$ and injective $g$ with $\pm c\in\operatorname{range}g$ where $\adjoin_\R\{\rho\circ g\}$
fails to separate points.
\item[C2.] $L^p$ density transfer: for finite Borel $\mu$ on compact metric $X$ and
$1\le p<\infty$, every point-separating EML subalgebra is dense in $L^p(\mu)$, via uniform
density $+$ the contraction $\norm{f}_{L^p}\le\mu(X)^{1/p}\norm{f}_\infty$ $+$ density of
$C(X)$ in $L^p$.
\item[C3.] Two-layer EML width bound on $\R^n$: every $f\in C(K,\R)$ on compact
$K\subseteq\R^n$ is approximable by $2n+1$ outer polynomials applied to single-activation
inner features $\sigma(\sum_i w_i x_i)$, realizing the Kolmogorov--Arnold width $2n+1$.
\item[C4.] Monotone-activation density equivalent to injectivity: for continuous $\sigma$,
$\sigma\circ g$ is universal for every compact $X$ and injective $g$ iff $\sigma$ is
injective.
\item[C5.] Quantitative degree/frequency bounds for exponential-polynomial approximation on
$[a,b]$ via the change-of-variable $+$ Jackson route of Section~\ref{sec:rates}.
\end{description}

